When you want to try something new, but all you can picture is people watching you get it wrong.
Sometimes the scary part isn't the thing itself.
It's the idea of looking silly while you learn.
So you'd rather not try at all than be seen as a beginner.

You only speak the new language inside your head.
You wait for a friend before going anywhere alone.
You skip the class until you feel "good enough" to show up.
You replay one awkward moment for days.

You should be able to be bad at something new without feeling ashamed.
You don't need to look good the first time.
But you do deserve the chance to get better.

Maybe it's not that you're shy or not brave enough.
Maybe you've been treating a normal part of being new like a big mistake.
Most people aren't watching as closely as you think.
And an awkward moment only stays with you as long as you keep carrying it.

Ask yourself:
What would I try this week if no one I knew would ever see it?
And:
Whose opinion am I actually afraid of?
